// THE WALK HANDS THE ELEMENT AND THE INDEX, EACH ITS OWN VALUE.
//
// A fixed array satisfies Range intrinsically (std/core/range.zen), so
// `xs.loop((h, i, v) ..)` lowers through lower_array_walk — a plain C for
// whose pass value is read STRAIGHT out of the member (`zg_elems[i]`),
// not called. Two bindings arrive at the body and they are different
// things: the counter and the element. Swap them and the program still
// compiles — the weights multiply whatever it is given.
//
// The weights make every plausible mix-up land on its own wrong number:
// hand the body the counter as the value (0,1,2,3) and the total is 14;
// hand it the element as the index (10,20,30,40) and it is 200. Only the
// true pairing also prints 200 -- which is why the body prints i beside
// v as well: neither half can be right alone, and any single-slot slip
// lands on its own row of the walk.

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    xs = [10, 20, 30, 40];
    total ::= 0;
    xs.loop((h, i, v) {
        total = total + i * v;
        println("{} {}", i, v);
    });
    println("{}", total);
    Ok(0);
}
